WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
Twenty-five tiny wells.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.440
Five positive ions down the side, five negative

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.680
ions across the top, and a few drops

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.200
of each in every well.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.200
Some wells stay clear.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.960
Some go cloudy: white, yellow, even brown.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.160
This grid is the evidence the solubility rules

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.040
come from.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:20.800
So how do you set it up so

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.000
the results can be trusted, and how do

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.280
you turn a grid of cloudy wells into

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:26.000
rules?

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.000
The clever part is the choice of solutions.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.000
Every positive ion comes as a nitrate: sodium,

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.280
calcium, barium, lead and silver nitrate.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.800
Every negative ion comes as a sodium salt:

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.520
sodium chloride, iodide, sulfate, carbonate and hydroxide.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.880
Nitrates and sodium compounds always dissolve.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.360
So when two solutions meet in a well,

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.360
the only pair that could form a solid

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:54.920
is the pair you're testing.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.600
And before you start, check each solution on

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:58.160
its own.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.440
It should be clear.

00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:02.586
Draw a five by five grid in your

00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:05.706
book and predict every cell first, using the

00:01:05.787 --> 00:01:07.346
rules from the last video.

00:01:07.627 --> 00:01:10.426
Predicting first is what turns your results into

00:01:10.507 --> 00:01:11.506
a test.

00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:14.026
Put a clear well plate over the grid.

00:01:14.587 --> 00:01:17.146
Add three drops of one positive ion solution

00:01:17.227 --> 00:01:20.106
along each row, then three drops of one

00:01:20.187 --> 00:01:22.426
negative ion solution down each column.

00:01:22.987 --> 00:01:24.786
Keep the dropper tip out of the well,

00:01:24.827 --> 00:01:26.426
so you don't contaminate the bottle.

00:01:26.827 --> 00:01:28.586
And look at each well over a black

00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:30.986
background as well as a white one, because

00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:33.146
a thin white cloud is hard to see

00:01:33.227 --> 00:01:33.866
on white.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.560
The independent variable is the pair of ions

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:38.900
in each well.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.740
The dependent variable is what you see: a

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.940
precipitate or not, its colour, and whether it's

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:47.020
a heavy solid or a faint cloud.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.700
Keep these controlled: every solution at the same

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:54.140
concentration, about nought point one moles per litre,

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:56.860
three drops of each, the same temperature, and

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.100
the same time before you record.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.180
Concentration matters most, because some compounds only just

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.980
dissolve, and whether they show a cloud depends

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.340
on how much is there.

00:02:08.567 --> 00:02:10.646
Here's what to expect, and you can check

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:13.206
each one against NAGSAG.

00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:16.486
The sodium row stays clear everywhere.

00:02:16.727 --> 00:02:19.126
The carbonate column gives a white solid with

00:02:19.207 --> 00:02:22.486
every metal except sodium, and silver's is pale

00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:23.246
yellow.

00:02:23.527 --> 00:02:26.326
Silver gives a solid with both halides, white

00:02:26.447 --> 00:02:29.846
silver chloride and pale yellow silver iodide, and

00:02:29.927 --> 00:02:32.246
lead gives bright yellow lead iodide.

00:02:32.727 --> 00:02:35.926
Barium and lead give white sulfates, while calcium

00:02:36.007 --> 00:02:38.806
and silver sulfate give only a faint cloud,

00:02:38.887 --> 00:02:39.526
if anything.

00:02:39.927 --> 00:02:42.966
And with hydroxide, silver gives a brown solid,

00:02:43.047 --> 00:02:44.646
but barium stays clear.

00:02:45.867 --> 00:02:48.146
Now turn the pattern into rules.

00:02:48.427 --> 00:02:51.186
Read along each row and down each column.

00:02:51.467 --> 00:02:54.346
The sodium row is clear in every well,

00:02:54.427 --> 00:02:57.226
so sodium compounds are soluble.

00:02:57.307 --> 00:03:00.186
The carbonate column has a solid everywhere except

00:03:00.267 --> 00:03:05.146
sodium, so carbonates are insoluble, except with sodium.

00:03:05.307 --> 00:03:07.866
Write each rule as a general statement, then

00:03:07.947 --> 00:03:10.426
the evidence for it, then any exception you

00:03:10.507 --> 00:03:11.386
saw.

00:03:11.467 --> 00:03:13.946
If you didn't test something, like ammonium or

00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:16.826
acetate, it isn't your conclusion.

00:03:16.907 --> 00:03:18.986
Note that it came from the reference table.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.400
Then compare your twenty-five predictions with your twenty-five

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:24.120
results.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.920
Every mismatch is either a rule you had

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.640
wrong or a technique problem, and you should

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:30.720
say which.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.360
If you predicted that barium hydroxide would precipitate,

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.160
the clear well corrects you: it's an exception.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.520
A faint cloud in calcium sulfate is real,

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.800
because some compounds are slightly soluble, a genuine

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:44.960
third category.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.000
But a faint cloud could also be a

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:50.040
dirty well, so repeat any doubtful cell in

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.000
a clean one.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.840
The syllabus also asks what's in the mixture

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:56.840
afterwards, and you can test that.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.520
Mix lead nitrate with potassium iodide, let the

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.160
yellow solid settle, and take some clear liquid

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.360
from above it.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.160
Add a drop of potassium iodide: if more

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.960
yellow solid forms, there were still lead ions

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.240
left, so the lead nitrate was in excess.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.880
Add a drop of lead nitrate instead: more

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.520
yellow means iodide was left over.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.880
The spectators, potassium and nitrate, are there either

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:22.360
way.

00:04:23.567 --> 00:04:25.806
The same three words come up in every

00:04:25.887 --> 00:04:27.406
practical question.

00:04:27.487 --> 00:04:30.766
It's valid because each well tests one pair,

00:04:30.847 --> 00:04:33.966
with partners that always dissolve, so a solid

00:04:34.087 --> 00:04:36.366
can only be the pair you're testing.

00:04:36.447 --> 00:04:39.726
It's reliable if repeated wells, and other groups,

00:04:39.807 --> 00:04:40.286
give the same

00:04:40.807 --> 00:04:41.433
results.

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:44.446
And it's accurate when your observations match what's

00:04:44.607 --> 00:04:47.806
really there: a black background, clean wells and

00:04:47.927 --> 00:04:49.566
fresh solutions all help.

00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:52.126
And the risk assessment.

00:04:52.207 --> 00:04:55.326
Lead nitrate is toxic, builds up in the

00:04:55.407 --> 00:04:57.806
body and can harm an unborn baby.

00:04:58.127 --> 00:05:01.726
Silver nitrate stains skin and can damage eyes.

00:05:01.807 --> 00:05:04.686
Barium nitrate is harmful if swallowed.

00:05:04.727 --> 00:05:07.566
Sodium hydroxide can harm eyes.

00:05:07.727 --> 00:05:10.926
So wear safety glasses and gloves, use only

00:05:11.007 --> 00:05:13.966
drops, never pipette by mouth, and wash your

00:05:14.127 --> 00:05:15.566
hands afterwards.

00:05:15.647 --> 00:05:18.126
Lead, silver and barium waste goes into a

00:05:18.207 --> 00:05:21.646
labelled heavy-metal container, never down the sink.

00:05:21.727 --> 00:05:24.526
And working with drops is itself a control:

00:05:24.727 --> 00:05:28.126
tiny amounts mean tiny exposure and tiny waste.

00:05:29.334 --> 00:05:30.813
Two lines to keep.

00:05:31.094 --> 00:05:34.453
Partners that always dissolve, so any solid is

00:05:34.494 --> 00:05:36.053
the pair you're testing.

00:05:36.134 --> 00:05:39.413
And the habit: predict first, then test, then

00:05:39.494 --> 00:05:41.573
explain every mismatch.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:43.720
Your turn.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.440
Potassium chloride solution is mixed with silver nitrate

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:50.240
solution, and a white solid settles.

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:52.880
A drop of potassium chloride is added to

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:55.360
the clear liquid above it, and more white

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:56.640
solid forms.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.320
Which ions were in that clear liquid?

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:00.960
Pause here to answer.

00:06:11.367 --> 00:06:15.086
Potassium, nitrate, and leftover silver ions.

00:06:15.367 --> 00:06:18.806
Adding chloride made more silver chloride, so silver

00:06:18.887 --> 00:06:21.606
ions must still have been dissolved: the silver

00:06:21.687 --> 00:06:23.126
nitrate was in excess.

00:06:23.687 --> 00:06:26.246
Potassium and nitrate are the spectators.

00:06:26.727 --> 00:06:29.606
It's not chloride: if chloride were in excess,

00:06:29.647 --> 00:06:31.766
the silver would be used up, and adding

00:06:31.887 --> 00:06:34.006
more chloride couldn't make more solid.

00:06:35.234 --> 00:06:35.873
Last one.

00:06:36.434 --> 00:06:40.033
Two groups mix calcium nitrate with sodium sulfate.

00:06:40.354 --> 00:06:42.913
One group records a faint white cloud.

00:06:43.154 --> 00:06:45.393
The other records no precipitate.

00:06:45.714 --> 00:06:48.113
Explain how both could happen, and how you'd

00:06:48.194 --> 00:06:49.713
make the result more reliable.

00:06:50.274 --> 00:06:51.553
Pause here to answer.

00:07:01.967 --> 00:07:05.486
Calcium sulfate is slightly soluble, so whether a

00:07:05.567 --> 00:07:08.206
cloud shows depends on how much is there.

00:07:08.767 --> 00:07:11.726
Different drop sizes, or not waiting long enough,

00:07:11.807 --> 00:07:13.966
can tip it either way, and a dirty

00:07:14.047 --> 00:07:15.486
well can fake a cloud.

00:07:16.047 --> 00:07:18.446
To make it reliable, repeat it in clean

00:07:18.527 --> 00:07:21.646
wells with the same concentrations, drops and waiting

00:07:21.727 --> 00:07:25.326
time, over black, and record "slight".

00:07:25.367 --> 00:07:27.486
Don't just decide one group was wrong.

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:32.780
So, to investigate solubility rules: choose partners that

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:37.340
always dissolve, predict every cell first, mix drops

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:39.900
in a clean well plate, view over black

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:42.860
and white, and repeat anything doubtful.

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:46.140
Turn patterns into rules with evidence, and explain

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:47.740
every mismatch.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:51.180
Next: Ksp, which puts a number on how

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:53.980
slightly soluble a compound really is.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:56.640
Here are the key notes.

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:58.800
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:58.880 --> 00:07:59.680
to copy them.
